  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 PM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Scattered convection will continue to develop and \ndeepen this afternoon over the srn half of Sampson Co. (and possibly \ninto the adjacent Sandhills and cntl Coastal Plain), with an \nisolated severe storm possible.\n\nA perturbation evident in GOES-E water vapor satellite data over the \nVirginias will pivot ewd and offshore through this evening, within a \nprogressive and lifting longwave trough axis trailing a mid/upr-\nlevel cyclone over srn QC. Related height falls and QG-forcing for \nascent will be focused along and offshore the Middle Atlantic coast, \nwith weakly rising heights forecast over cntl NC. Preceding that \nperturbation, 35-45 kts of mid-level wswly flow now peaking over e-\ncntl through nern NC will gradually weaken through this evening-\novernight. \n\nAt the surface, the sea breeze and outflow will precede a front now \nstretching from near the Albemarle Sound swwd to near LBT and FLO, \nwhich will remain quasi-stationary through the evening and early \novernight. The front will then be nudged coast-ward Tue morning, as \npressure rises preceding a 1019-1020 mb high over the OH Valley and \nMidwest progress east of the Appalachians. \n\nVisible satellite, radar, and surface observational trends indicate \nthe main severe risk over cntl NC will be over the srn half of \nSampson Co. through this evening, nearest a maximum of 2500-4500 \nJ/kg MLCAPE over sern NC, per the 18Z-observed MHX RAOB and SPC \nmesoanalysis data. Thus far, cells have been mostly of the \npulse/multi-cell variety and not exhibited any appreciable rotation, \nsuggestive that they have either not yet deepened sufficiently to \ninteract with the environmental shear noted above or may be \ndisplaced slightly swd of the stronger flow/shear over nern NC. In \neither event, damaging wet microbursts will pose the primary hazard, \nas water and melting hail-loaded downdrafts collapse and become \ndiabatically-enhanced within a high DCAPE (1000-1500 K/kg) and steep \n0-3 km lapse rate environment (8.5-9 C/km). Should any cells begin \nto interact with the environmental shear and consequently display at \nleast transient rotation/supercellular characteristics, then a risk \nof severe hail would also result. \n\nWhile drier, nwly flow and shallow cumulus has otherwise been \npresent this afternoon immediately northwest of the surface front, \nany outflow that surges to the nw of the ongoing convection may \nproduce isolated, weaker cells in a weakly unstable environment \nthrough the evening. \n\nIt will otherwise be seasonably mild and with comfortable humidity \nlevels throughout the rest of cntl NC, with low temperatures in the \nmid/upr 50s in rural Piedmont locations to 60s elsewhere. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Diurnally driven storms expected to return from mid \nweek into the weekend, with the greatest chances on Thursday and \nFriday. \n\nA longwave low amplitude H5 trough over the Ohio Valley will \ngradually slide eastward by the middle of the week, as the influence \nof offshore ridging gradually wanes across the region. This will \nallow for a gradual increase in moisture as weak southerly flow \nimports anomalously high PW's into the area as early as \nWednesday/Thursday. Meanwhile, the longwave trough and attendant \nsurface cold front will approach the area from the northwest on \nThursday, serving as a focal point for shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment area-wide. It would appear the greatest storm chances on \nThursday would be across the western Piedmont in closest proximity \nto the front and weak Piedmont trough, although pretty much anywhere \nacross central NC will have increased storm chances given abundant \nPW's and MLCAPEs around 1000-1500 J/kG. \n\nAdditional shower and thunderstorm chances are expected area-wide on \nFriday as the surface front slides into NC.  How far southward this \nfront makes it before it stalls out is likely the biggest piece of \nforecast uncertainty that we're encountering today. The lack of \nstrong post-frontal northwesterly flow aloft would suggest the front \nwill likely stall over NC, then buckle back northward or wash out \ncompletely this weekend. Naturally this would allow for a continued \nperiod of afternoon shower and thunderstorm chances. With the flow \naloft gradually taking on a more zonal component this weekend, \nconvection within the Piedmont trough as well as sea breeze induced \nstorms seem like the most likely outcome.",
